interactive_layout:
# Explore a Composition Law That Remembers Parentheses

Begin with the operation $a\star b=b$ when $a=0$, $a\star b=a$ when $b=0$, and $a\star b=a+2b$ otherwise. The identity behaves conventionally; associativity does not.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

Use the laboratory to discover where the two bracketings diverge. For positive inputs the discrepancy has a simple exact law.

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the positive defect calculation</summary>
For $a,b,c>0$, the left bracketing is $a+2b+2c$, while the right bracketing is $a+2b+4c$. Their difference is therefore $2c$.
</details>

{{visualization:0}}

## The smallest witness

Three copies of $1$ produce $5$ and $7$. They are not equal; the key move is to connect them by a reversible arrow one dimension higher.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{demo:0}}

For background on this shift from equality to structured equivalence, see the [nLab overview of bicategories](https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/bicategory) and the [Stanford Encyclopedia discussion of category theory](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/category-theory/).

## Five bracketings, one coherent pentagon

Four inputs have five binary bracketings. Their number is the Catalan number $C_3=5$.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{interactive_demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal why both routes agree</summary>
Every edge is an associator two-cell. Since there is exactly one two-cell between any fixed pair of one-cells, any two path composites with the same endpoints must be equal. This proves the pentagon and, by the same reasoning, the triangle law.
</details>

## Audit the structure

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the strictness obstruction</summary>
A strict structure on the fixed composition would require $(1\star1)\star1=1\star(1\star1)$. Direct evaluation turns this into $5=7$, which is impossible. The associator is therefore essential rather than decorative.
</details>

## Continue experimenting

{{demo:2}}

The construction invites generalization to arbitrary unital magmas, nontrivial two-cell symmetry groups, cocycle-valued associators, and strictification up to biequivalence.

